-- Breaking BCS: 시작 기사 20건 (영문) — 바로 발행 (대표님 요청), Opinion 은 작성 중(초안)
-- Supabase SQL 에디터에서 실행 (seed-breakingbcs.sql → breakingbcs-editor.sql 다음). 여러 번 실행해도 된다 (같은 제목 기사는 건너뛴다)
--   · Bryan·College Station 시·카운티·학교구, Texas A&M 공식 발표, 미국 연방·텍사스 주 기관 자료를 바탕으로 AI가 새로 쓴 기사. 본문 끝에 AI 활용 표시
--   · 기자명: 편집장 John Lee. 본문 첫머리는 미국 신문식 데이트라인(예: COLLEGE STATION —)
--     John Lee 계정이 있으면 작성자도 그 계정 (없으면 이 매체 편집장 → 관리자 → 총관리자 계정. 기자명은 어느 경우나 John Lee)
--   · 사진: 저장소 /sites/breakingbcs/photos/ (위키미디어 공용 공개 라이선스 자료사진, 배포가 끝난 뒤에 보인다)
--   · 기사마다 확인할 점·원문 주소·이용 조건을 AI 메모(편집 메모)에 남겼다

with ctx as (
  select o.id as outlet_id,
         coalesce(
           (select p.id from profiles p where p.full_name = 'John Lee' order by (p.outlet_id = o.id) desc nulls last, p.created_at limit 1),
           (select p.id from profiles p where p.outlet_id = o.id and p.role = 'editor' order by p.created_at limit 1),
           (select p.id from profiles p where p.outlet_id = o.id and p.role = 'admin' order by p.created_at limit 1),
           (select p.id from profiles p where (to_jsonb(p) ->> 'is_super') = 'true' order by p.created_at limit 1)
         ) as author_id
  from outlets o where o.name = 'Breaking BCS'
  order by o.created_at limit 1
)
insert into articles (outlet_id, category_id, author_id, title, body, excerpt, thumbnail_url, status, published_at, tags, byline, ai_notes)
select ctx.outlet_id, c.id, ctx.author_id, v.title, v.body, v.excerpt, v.thumb, v.status::article_status, v.published_at, v.tags, 'John Lee', v.notes
from ctx
cross join (values
  ('business', 'published', 'Bryan, College Station October Sales Tax Payments Up About 8% From 2025', '<p>COLLEGE STATION — Bryan and College Station each received more local sales tax revenue from the state in October than a year earlier, though both trailed the statewide gain for Texas cities, according to Texas Comptroller of Public Accounts allocation data.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs-bz-03.webp" alt="A &quot;Welcome to College Station&quot; sign along northbound State Highway 6 (file photo, 2007). Photo: Blueag9 / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 2.5)"><p><em>▲ A "Welcome to College Station" sign along northbound State Highway 6 (file photo, 2007). Photo: Blueag9 / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 2.5)</em></p><p>College Station''s October payment was $3,818,058, up 8.79% from $3,509,533 in October 2025. Bryan received $2,615,237, up 8.08% from $2,419,662, the data show. Each city levies a 1.5% local sales tax rate, according to the comptroller''s figures.</p><p>The comptroller collects local sales taxes along with the state''s share and sends cities their portion each month. The October allocations are based on sales tax collected in September, mostly from businesses that file monthly and reported their August sales, the agency said in an Oct. 8 news release.</p><p>Statewide, the comptroller distributed $1.3 billion in local sales tax for October, up 11.6% from a year earlier. Allocations to cities totaled $796.0 million, an 11.3% increase.</p><p>For the calendar year through October, College Station has received $35,039,962, up 5.59% from the same period of 2025. Bryan''s total is $27,072,762, up 5.85%. Statewide, city allocations are up 6.7% for the year to date.</p><p>Both cities'' totals rose after a mixed start to the year. College Station''s January and February payments were slightly below 2025 levels, and Bryan''s February payment was 15.82% lower than a year earlier, before larger gains in the spring and summer.</p><p>Sales tax is a major source of general fund revenue for both cities, which use it alongside property taxes to pay for services. In the release, Comptroller Don Huffines said "the Texas economy is strong" and called rising revenue "an opportunity for local elected officials to reduce property taxes."</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'State allocation data show College Station received about $3.82 million and Bryan about $2.62 million in local sales tax in October, gains that trailed the statewide increase for cities.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs-bz-03.webp', '2026-10-09T09:40:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['sales tax','Texas Comptroller','Bryan','College Station','city budgets']::text[], '• 확인할 점: City figures come from the comptroller''s ''Sales Tax Allocation, City-County Comparison Summary'' dataset on data.texas.gov (report month 10, year 2026, updated Oct. 7, 2026). Dollar amounts rounded to the nearest dollar.
• 확인할 점: Comptroller quote: the release opens with "The Texas economy is strong" (capital T, start of a longer sentence); rendered lowercase mid-sentence. Editor may prefer to restructure.
• 확인할 점: The paragraph on sales tax as a general fund source is general background; confirm wording against each city''s FY2027 budget if desired.
• 확인할 점: Year-to-date figures are calendar-year (January-October payments), not the cities'' Oct. 1 fiscal year.
• 확인할 점: Photo author on Commons is ''Blueag9 assumed (based on copyright claims)''; file from 2007.
• 원문(Texas Comptroller — Comptroller Huffines Distributes $1.3 Billion in Monthly Sales Tax Revenue to Local Governments (Oct. 8, 2026)): https://comptroller.texas.gov/about/media-center/news/20261008-comptroller-huffines-distributes-13-billion-in-monthly-sales-tax-revenue-to-local-governments-1791392487613
• 원문(Texas Comptroller via data.texas.gov — Sales Tax Allocation, City-County Comparison Summary, Brazos County cities, 2026): https://data.texas.gov/resource/53pa-m7sm.json?$where=county=''Brazos'' AND report_year=''2026''
• 이용 조건: Text: original writing based on Texas Comptroller public release and open data (data.texas.gov) / Photo: CC BY 2.5, Blueag9, File:WelcometoCollegeStation.JPG
• 발행: 대표님 요청으로 승인 대기 없이 바로 발행'),
  ('business', 'published', 'Bryan Agreement Sets Path to Restore Two Historic Downtown Buildings', '<p>BRYAN — Two historic buildings on South Main Street in downtown Bryan are set to be restored under a 10-year economic development agreement, one of several business projects the City of Bryan highlighted in an Oct. 15 summary of its 2026 fiscal year.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs-bz-02.webp" alt="The 1910 Brazos Union Masonic Lodge building in Bryan (file photo, 2014; not one of the buildings in the agreement). Photo: Larry D. Moore / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 4.0)"><p><em>▲ The 1910 Brazos Union Masonic Lodge building in Bryan (file photo, 2014; not one of the buildings in the agreement). Photo: Larry D. Moore / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 4.0)</em></p><p>The Bryan City Council approved the Chapter 380 agreement with NN Out Properties at a Sept. 15 special meeting, the city said. Chapter 380 of the Texas Local Government Code allows cities to offer grants or loans to promote economic development. The deal covers the Hotel Bryan and the Ryan Building at 211 and 217 S. Main St.</p><p>Under the plan, the Hotel Bryan''s ground floors would hold retail and other targeted tenant space, with loft space on the upper floors. The agreement sets deadlines: the developer must close on both buildings by Dec. 1, 2026, start construction on the Ryan Building by April 1, 2027, and begin upper-floor work at the Hotel Bryan by Sept. 1, 2027. The city''s summary did not state the dollar value of the city''s incentives.</p><p>The summary also pointed to a $298 million research and computing project at Texas A&amp;M''s RELLIS campus. Construction stopped in November 2025 after the original developer filed for bankruptcy, and in June the council advanced a replacement agreement with the new owner, ThisWay Global, which is expected to resume construction, the city said.</p><p>Other projects listed include a $110 million expansion by manufacturer Axis Pipe &amp; Tube, which the city identifies as its largest taxpayer, under an agreement approved in December 2025, and an equipment expansion at Centex Roto Molding in Bryan Business Park that requires at least a $5 million increase in property value and 50 additional full-time jobs.</p><p>In August, the Bryan-Brazos County Economic Development Foundation sold 56.9 acres in North Bryan to Kristen Distributing, which plans a distribution facility of up to 200,000 square feet and must begin construction within 24 months. Industrial gas company Messer Americas is building an air separation unit expected to create 16 full-time jobs and to be finished in late 2027, the city said.</p><p>Downtown, construction began this fiscal year on The Lumin, a building with five floors of office space and ground-floor restaurant and retail space at Lake Walk, along with a two-story parking garage. It is being developed with Bryan Commerce &amp; Development Inc., the city''s economic development arm, and is expected to open in 2027.</p><p>"We are open for business in Bryan, which is a win for everyone in our community," Mayor Bobby Gutierrez said in the summary. Todd McDaniel, the city''s director of economic development, said, "We offer abundant access to land, water, electricity and an impressive talent pipeline."</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'A 10-year Chapter 380 agreement targets the Hotel Bryan and Ryan Building on South Main Street, one of several business projects the City of Bryan highlighted for fiscal 2026.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs-bz-02.webp', '2026-10-16T10:15:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['Downtown Bryan','economic development','Chapter 380','RELLIS','Bryan']::text[], '• 확인할 점: City post gives the council approval as ''the Sept. 15 Special Meeting'' without a year; context (FY2026 summary, Dec. 1, 2026 closing deadline) indicates 2026. Confirm in Bryan council minutes.
• 확인할 점: Dollar value of the downtown Chapter 380 incentives was not in the release; check the Sept. 15 agenda packet if the editor wants to add it.
• 확인할 점: Left out the release''s line that Axis Pipe & Tube''s plan ''will bring $4 million worth of new jobs'' because the meaning is unclear; verify with the city before using.
• 확인할 점: Left out the Amazon delivery station, 26th & Main restoration and downtown restaurant/distillery mentions to avoid promoting individual businesses; all are in the source if wanted.
• 확인할 점: The ThisWay Global project is described by the city as a ''Research and AI Compute Center'' at RELLIS; project status may change.
• 확인할 점: Photo is a 2014 file photo of a different downtown Bryan building.
• 원문(City of Bryan — 2026 Full of Economic Development Wins for the City of Bryan & Residents (Oct. 15, 2026)): https://www.bryantx.gov/news/posts/2026-full-of-economic-development-wins-for-the-city-of-bryan-residents/
• 이용 조건: Text: original writing based on a City of Bryan public news release; quotes as published / Photo: CC BY 4.0, Larry D. Moore, File:Bryan tx masonic bldg 2014.jpg
• 발행: 대표님 요청으로 승인 대기 없이 바로 발행'),
  ('business', 'published', 'College Station-Bryan Unemployment Rate Falls to 3.5% in September', '<p>COLLEGE STATION — The unemployment rate in the College Station-Bryan metropolitan area fell to 3.5% in September, among the lowest in Texas, according to estimates the Texas Workforce Commission released Oct. 16.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs-bz-04.webp" alt="The pavilion in College Station&#x27;s Northgate district (file photo, 2009). Photo: Oldag07 / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 3.0)"><p><em>▲ The pavilion in College Station''s Northgate district (file photo, 2009). Photo: Oldag07 / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 3.0)</em></p><p>The rate was down from 4.0% in August and from 3.7% in September 2025. The figures are not seasonally adjusted, meaning they include normal seasonal swings such as the return of university students and staff in late summer.</p><p>The metro area, which includes Brazos, Burleson and Robertson counties, had a civilian labor force of about 156,200 people in September. About 150,800 were employed and about 5,500 were unemployed, according to the commission, the state agency that oversees unemployment benefits and labor market data.</p><p>Midland had the lowest unemployment rate among Texas metro areas at 3.2%. College Station-Bryan tied with Abilene, Amarillo and San Angelo at 3.5%. The statewide rate was 4.3% before seasonal adjustment and 4.4% after.</p><p>A separate count of jobs at local employers, produced by the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics with the state, put total nonfarm employment in the metro area at 147,000 in September, a preliminary figure. That was up from 145,600 a year earlier and from 142,300 in August.</p><p>Government employers, a category that includes public schools and universities, accounted for 51,100 of those jobs in September, compared with 46,800 in August and 50,100 a year earlier, according to BLS data.</p><p>Statewide, Texas had 14,461,300 nonfarm jobs in September after losing 11,100 positions during the month, but added 154,300 jobs over the year, a growth rate of 1.1%, the commission said. "The addition of more than 154,000 jobs over the year reflects balanced growth across Texas industries," TWC Chairman Joe Esparza said.</p><p>The commission said all of its estimates are subject to revision.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'The Texas Workforce Commission says the College Station-Bryan metro jobless rate fell to 3.5% in September, among the lowest of Texas'' metro areas, as local payrolls grew from a year ago.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs-bz-04.webp', '2026-10-17T11:05:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['unemployment','jobs','Texas Workforce Commission','College Station-Bryan metro','economy']::text[], '• 확인할 점: Metro rates are from the TWC Oct. 16, 2026 release table (not seasonally adjusted, in thousands). As of Oct. 27 the BLS LAUS API still showed August (4.0%, preliminary) as the latest metro rate, so BLS will publish its own September figure later; numbers may be revised.
• 확인할 점: The TWC release states the next release (for September) is Nov. 20, 2026 — this appears to be an error in the release (likely October data); not mentioned in the article.
• 확인할 점: Nonfarm and government payroll figures are from BLS State and Metro Area Employment series SMU48177800000000001 and SMU48177809000000001 (not seasonally adjusted; September 2026 preliminary).
• 확인할 점: Metro-area county list (Brazos, Burleson, Robertson) is the standard OMB definition; confirm it is unchanged for 2026 data.
• 확인할 점: Article avoids attributing the August-to-September increase to Texas A&M specifically, since BLS does not break out the university.
• 확인할 점: Photo: Commons page lists GFDL / CC BY-SA 3.0 / Free Art License (multi-license); CC BY-SA 3.0 chosen. Thumbnail not visually checked for signs or faces — editor should look before publishing.
• 원문(Texas Workforce Commission — Texas Annual Growth Rate Outpaces Nation as Labor Force Adds 15,800 (Oct. 16, 2026)): https://www.twc.texas.gov/news/texas-annual-growth-rate-outpaces-nation-labor-force-adds-15800
• 원문(U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics — CES total nonfarm, College Station-Bryan MSA (SMU48177800000000001), accessed Oct. 27, 2026): https://api.bls.gov/publicAPI/v2/timeseries/data/SMU48177800000000001
• 원문(U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics — CES government, College Station-Bryan MSA (SMU48177809000000001), accessed Oct. 27, 2026): https://api.bls.gov/publicAPI/v2/timeseries/data/SMU48177809000000001
• 이용 조건: Text: original writing based on a TWC public release and BLS data (U.S. federal work, public domain); quote as published by TWC / Photo: CC BY-SA 3.0, Oldag07, File:Northgate pavillion.jpg
• 발행: 대표님 요청으로 승인 대기 없이 바로 발행'),
  ('bryan', 'published', 'Bryan Lists 2026 Park Upgrades, With Midtown Legacy Playground Still to Come', '<p>BRYAN — The city of Bryan says it added new playgrounds, shade covers, trail connections and ballpark improvements across its parks system this year, with more projects scheduled to finish in the coming months.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs-ln-04.webp" alt="The entrance to Boonville Heritage Park in Bryan (file photo, 2018). It is not one of the parks named in the city&#x27;s 2026 improvement list. Photo: Larry D. Moore / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 4.0)"><p><em>▲ The entrance to Boonville Heritage Park in Bryan (file photo, 2018). It is not one of the parks named in the city''s 2026 improvement list. Photo: Larry D. Moore / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 4.0)</em></p><p>Claude Scurry Park and Copperfield Park each received new playgrounds built for children ages 5 to 12, and Ibarra Park got a playground for ages 2 to 5, the city said in a news release Oct. 19. New shade structures went up at Sue Haswell Park, Tanglewood Park and over the fitness station at Greenbrier Park.</p><p>Two shaded playgrounds are being built at Villa West Park to replace equipment that had been in use for about 25 years. The city expects them to be finished by the end of the year.</p><p>Other work focused on safety and durability. Crews replaced the decking on the large pedestrian bridge at Austin''s Colony Park, repaired bridge railings on Turkey Creek Trail and replaced eroding granite paths at Morris "Buzz" Hamilton Dog Park with concrete. A new trail connection with a crosswalk now links Thornberry Drive and Teller Drive in the Austin''s Colony subdivision. Two pedestrian bridges for Bonham Park are scheduled for delivery in January 2027.</p><p>At Travis Major Field, which the city began managing fully on Jan. 1, 2026, an almost $700,000 renovation rebuilt the dugouts and a party deck and added new turf, netting and a sound system, among other upgrades, the city said.</p><p>At Travis Bryan Midtown Park, the boardwalk and the last sections of a 12-foot-wide trail are expected to open this fall, completing an almost two-mile loop. Construction continues on Midtown Legacy Park, a playground designed for all ages and abilities that is supported by a $2 million donation from The Darwood Foundation. The city expects it to open in late 2026 or early 2027 with 119 play components and more than 12,000 square feet of shade.</p><p>"From installing new playgrounds and shade structures, to upgrading walking paths and bridges, we''ve made significant progress in improving Bryan''s parks this year," Mayor Bobby Gutierrez said in the release.</p><p>The Arbor Day Foundation also named Bryan a Tree City USA Community for the 37th straight year, and city staff planted 100 trees donated by the Bryan Noon Lions Club, the city said.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'Bryan says new playgrounds, shade structures, trail links and a nearly $700,000 Travis Major Field renovation were finished this year, with a Midtown Park playground expected by early 2027.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs-ln-04.webp', '2026-10-20T10:30:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['Bryan','parks','Travis Bryan Midtown Park','playgrounds']::text[], '• 확인할 점: Mayor Bobby Gutierrez is a candidate in the Nov. 3 mayoral election; the quote comes from an official city release. Editor may wish to cut or balance it during the election period.
• 확인할 점: Boonville Heritage Park may not be city-owned; the caption does not claim it is a city park. Replace with a photo of a park named in the story if available.
• 확인할 점: Opening dates for Villa West playgrounds, Midtown trail loop and Midtown Legacy Park are city estimates and may slip.
• 확인할 점: Travis Major Field renovation is described by the city as ''almost $700,000''; itemized costs not given.
• 원문(City of Bryan — City of Bryan Highlights 2026 Improvements Across Parks & Recreation System (Oct. 19, 2026)): https://www.bryantx.gov/news/posts/city-of-bryan-highlights-2026-improvements-across-parks-recreation-system/
• 이용 조건: Text: original reporting from a City of Bryan public news release (facts rewritten; one quote from the release) / Photo: CC BY 4.0, Larry D. Moore, File:Boonville Heritage Park Entrance.jpg
• 발행: 대표님 요청으로 승인 대기 없이 바로 발행'),
  ('food-dining', 'published', 'Drought Cuts Texas Pumpkin Yields by Up to 40% Ahead of Holiday Baking Season', '<p>COLLEGE STATION — Fewer Texas-grown pumpkins are reaching porches and pie plates this fall. Extreme summer drought cut yields in the state''s main pumpkin-growing area by an estimated 30% to 40%, according to the Texas A&amp;M AgriLife Extension Service.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs-ls-04.webp" alt="Pumpkins (file photo, 2009; location not specified, not a Texas harvest photo). Photo: Michael Kooiman / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 3.0)"><p><em>▲ Pumpkins (file photo, 2009; location not specified, not a Texas harvest photo). Photo: Michael Kooiman / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 3.0)</em></p><p>The estimate comes from Mark Carroll, the AgriLife Extension agriculture and natural resources agent for Floyd County. The county sits east of Lubbock on the Texas High Plains, is the designated Pumpkin Capital of Texas and has been the state''s leading pumpkin-producing area for several years, Carroll said in the agency''s Oct. 6 crop and weather report.</p><p>"We were in an extreme drought throughout the summer," Carroll said. "No rainfall for the pumpkin crop, and it greatly affected our yields." He said 2026 is the driest year he has seen in Floyd County in 20 years as an Extension agent.</p><p>Texas growers planted 1,200 acres of pumpkins this year, Carroll said, and statewide acreage usually ranges from 700 to 1,600 acres. All of it is irrigated, mostly with drip systems. Irrigation could not make up for the drought because fields started the season with little or no moisture in the subsoil, he said.</p><p>The dry weather brought one benefit. Pumpkins are highly prone to fungal disease, and growers usually spray several times a season, but dry conditions meant fewer applications, Carroll said.</p><p>The harvest usually starts around Sept. 1. This year it was expected to finish in the week of the report, one to two weeks earlier than normal, because there were fewer pumpkins to pick.</p><p>Demand peaks around Halloween and Thanksgiving, when people buy pumpkins for decorating and baking, according to the report. Other states, including Illinois, grow more pumpkins by volume, but much of that crop is processed into products such as canned pumpkin. Texas growers instead focus on decorative and culinary pumpkins, with more than 200 varieties that include jack-o''-lantern, pie and Cinderella types.</p><p>AgriLife Extension is the outreach arm of the Texas A&amp;M University System. It publishes the statewide crop and weather report, which compiles conditions reported from each of its 12 districts.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'Extreme summer drought cut pumpkin yields in Texas'' top growing area by an estimated 30% to 40%, Texas A&M AgriLife Extension reports, and harvest ended early this fall.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs-ls-04.webp', '2026-10-21T14:10:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['pumpkins','drought','Texas A&M AgriLife','Thanksgiving','Texas agriculture']::text[], '• 확인할 점: The local hook is weak. The source is a statewide report from the College Station-based Texas A&M AgriLife Extension Service, and no Brazos County food event from the city, university or health district could be confirmed. City of College Station and Bryan news (Senior Autumn Fest, Restaurant Week in August) did not fit the time window or the no-price rule, and the Brazos County Health District site was blocked. Swap this piece if an editor has a better local food item.
• 확인할 점: The ''Texas A&M University System outreach arm'' description and ''College Station-based'' are general knowledge and were not confirmed on a page opened for this article.
• 확인할 점: Carroll''s statement that pumpkin prices have held steady for three years was left out to follow the no-prices rule.
• 확인할 점: The photo location is unknown (Commons ''own work'', Oct. 24, 2009). The caption says the location is not specified. The original is exactly 500 px wide, the minimum allowed.
• 확인할 점: The quote matches the AgriLife Today article exactly, except that the source uses curly quotation marks.
• 원문(Texas A&M AgriLife Today — Drought conditions cut Texas pumpkin yields (Oct. 6, 2026)): https://agrilifetoday.tamu.edu/2026/10/06/drought-conditions-cut-texas-pumpkin-yields/
• 이용 조건: Text: original wording based on facts from Texas A&M AgriLife Today (official Texas A&M news). One short attributed quote. / Photo: CC BY-SA 3.0 (also GFDL), Michael Kooiman, File:Pumpkins-2009.jpg
• 발행: 대표님 요청으로 승인 대기 없이 바로 발행'),
  ('latest', 'published', 'College Station Council Awards Contracts for Jones Butler Road, Sewer Line', '<p>COLLEGE STATION — The College Station City Council approved contracts Thursday, Oct. 22, for two large public works projects: the Jones Butler Road extension and roundabout, and the next phase of the Northeast Sewer Trunk Line.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs-ln-02.webp" alt="Farm to Market Road 2818 in College Station, the southern end of the planned Jones Butler Road work (file photo, March 2026). It is not a photo of the project site. Photo: TBKS1 / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 4.0)"><p><em>▲ Farm to Market Road 2818 in College Station, the southern end of the planned Jones Butler Road work (file photo, March 2026). It is not a photo of the project site. Photo: TBKS1 / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 4.0)</em></p><p>Both contracts passed unanimously as part of the consent agenda, according to the city''s live blog of the meeting.</p><p>Brazos Paving received a $12.34 million contract for the Jones Butler Extension and Roundabout Project. With a $1.25 million city contingency, the total approval is $13.59 million. The project rebuilds Jones Butler Road from FM 2818 to Holleman Drive South and includes a proposed multi-lane roundabout at North Dowling Road, plus water transmission lines for the Dowling Road Pump Station, the city said. The council also added $913,181 to the design contract with Kimley-Horn and Associates, which includes a specialized construction inspector.</p><p>Cash Construction received a contract of about $13.6 million for Phase 3 of the Northeast Sewer Trunk Line, with a $1 million contingency. The trunk line serves Northgate, Hensel Park, Century Square, Chimney Hill and areas from FM 60 south to the Carters Creek Wastewater Treatment Plant. Phase 3 replaces the line from south of Harvey Road to west of State Highway 6, and the project adds capacity and moves the line away from the creek bank, the city said.</p><p>The council also approved preliminary engineering work tied to moving the railroad crossing at Capstone to line up with Barron Road, including an agreement with Union Pacific Railroad for an estimated $60,000 reimbursement by the city.</p><p>In a separate vote, the council unanimously amended its Unified Development Ordinance to define data centers — structures or portions larger than 5,000 square feet used mainly for computer and network equipment — and to set the zoning districts where they may be allowed. Through the conditional use permit process, the council may add conditions such as buffering, screening, lighting and noise limits.</p><p>The council also unanimously approved the rezoning for the Savannah Oaks master-planned development in south College Station and voted 6-1 to adopt a new parks master plan. The council next meets Monday, Nov. 16.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'College Station approved about $28 million in contracts Oct. 22 to rebuild Jones Butler Road with a roundabout and replace a section of the Northeast Sewer Trunk Line.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs-ln-02.webp', '2026-10-23T08:20:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['College Station','City Council','Jones Butler Road','infrastructure','data centers']::text[], '• 확인할 점: Sewer contract amount differs between city sources: the Oct. 21 preview says $13.65 million ($14.65 million total); the Oct. 22 live blog says $13.6 million ($14.6 million total). Story uses ''about $13.6 million'' — confirm against the agenda packet or minutes. The excerpt''s ''about $28 million'' is the sum of the two totals (~$28.2M).
• 확인할 점: Live blog says it is ''not the official minutes''; confirm with official minutes once posted.
• 확인할 점: Construction start dates and timelines were not given in the city sources and are left out.
• 확인할 점: Photo caption describes FM 2818 as the southern end of the project because the city says the work runs from FM 2818 to Holleman Drive South; the exact spot in the photo is not known.
• 확인할 점: Savannah Oaks is covered in depth by the business desk (bcs-bz-01); mentioned here in one line only.
• 원문(City of College Station blog — Live from City Hall: Thursday''s City Council meeting (Oct. 22, 2026)): https://blog.cstx.gov/2026/10/22/live-from-city-hall-thursdays-city-council-meeting-oct-22/
• 원문(City of College Station blog — 5 things to watch at Thursday''s City Council meeting (Oct. 21, 2026)): https://blog.cstx.gov/2026/10/21/5-things-to-watch-at-thursdays-city-council-meeting-103/
• 이용 조건: Text: original reporting from City of College Station public blog posts (facts rewritten) / Photo: CC BY 4.0, TBKS1, File:Farm to Market Road 2818 in College Station, TX.jpg
• 발행: 대표님 요청으로 승인 대기 없이 바로 발행'),
  ('health-sleep', 'published', 'CDC Says Get Flu Shots by End of October; Texas A&M Offers Campus Vaccines', '<p>COLLEGE STATION — As October ends, federal health officials say people should ideally have their flu vaccine by the end of the month. Texas A&amp;M University says it offered shots to students, faculty and staff across its campuses in September and October.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs-ls-02.webp" alt="A Navy hospital corpsman checks the dose of an influenza vaccine aboard the aircraft carrier USS Abraham Lincoln (file photo, 2002; not taken in the Brazos Valley). Photo: Philip A. McDaniel, U.S. Navy / Wikimedia Commons (public domain). Face blurred by Breaking BCS."><p><em>▲ A Navy hospital corpsman checks the dose of an influenza vaccine aboard the aircraft carrier USS Abraham Lincoln (file photo, 2002; not taken in the Brazos Valley). Photo: Philip A. McDaniel, U.S. Navy / Wikimedia Commons (public domain). Face blurred by Breaking BCS.</em></p><p>The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention says everyone 6 months and older in the United States, with rare exceptions, should get a flu vaccine every season. The agency calls September and October generally good months to be vaccinated and says that, ideally, everyone should be vaccinated by the end of October.</p><p>Timing matters because protection is not immediate. The CDC says it takes about two weeks after vaccination for the body to develop antibodies, and it recommends getting vaccinated before flu viruses begin spreading in the community. Some children need two doses given at least four weeks apart, so the agency recommends they get the first dose as soon as vaccine is available.</p><p>For the 2026-27 season, every flu vaccine will be trivalent, meaning it protects against three flu strains, according to the CDC. The agency says adults 65 and older have three preferred vaccine options and should talk with their provider about which one to get.</p><p>Flu vaccines are available at doctor''s offices, clinics, health departments, pharmacies, urgent care clinics and college health centers, and sometimes at schools and workplaces, according to the CDC.</p><p>Texas A&amp;M University Health Services, the campus health center, says on its influenza page that vaccines are available to students, staff and faculty across Texas A&amp;M campuses during September and October 2026, by appointment. The center asks patients to bring a Texas A&amp;M ID and says students are encouraged, but not required, to share health insurance information. It says it uses seasonal flu campaign funds to cover students who have no insurance.</p><p>The health center, drawing on CDC guidance, also lists everyday ways to slow the flu''s spread: avoid close contact with sick people, cover coughs and sneezes, wash hands often with soap and water, avoid touching the eyes, nose and mouth, and clean surfaces that may be contaminated. People who have the flu should stay home and avoid others except to get medical care. Anyone with emergency warning signs should seek care right away.</p><p>Health Services also notes that antiviral drugs may be a treatment option and advises anyone who thinks they have the flu to check with their primary care provider.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'The CDC recommends a flu vaccine for nearly everyone 6 months and older, ideally by the end of October. Texas A&M is offering shots to students, faculty and staff this fall.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs-ls-02.webp', '2026-10-23T10:40:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['flu vaccine','CDC','Texas A&M','University Health Services','public health']::text[], '• 확인할 점: The Brazos County Health District website (brazoshealth.org) returned 403 and blocked automated requests, so none of the district''s 2026 clinics, dates or announcements could be confirmed. The district is left out of the article. If an editor can confirm its fall 2026 flu clinic details on the official site, they could be added.
• 확인할 점: COVID-19 vaccine guidance was left out. The CDC COVID vaccine page could not be read, and the federal recommendations have changed recently, so they should be checked before any are added.
• 확인할 점: UHS says vaccines were available during ''September and October 2026'', so the campus offer may end Oct. 31. The article does not say whether appointments are still open. Check the UHS page before publishing.
• 확인할 점: The CDC Key Facts page was last updated Sept. 1, 2026, and notes that ''Updates to this page are forthcoming''. Recheck for changes.
• 확인할 점: The CDC page does not say whether vaccination after October still helps, so the article makes no claim about that.
• 확인할 점: Phone numbers and the appointment link were left out on purpose.
• 원문(CDC — Key Facts About Seasonal Flu Vaccine (updated Sept. 1, 2026)): https://www.cdc.gov/flu/vaccines/keyfacts.html
• 원문(Texas A&M University Health Services — Influenza (2026)): https://uhs.tamu.edu/prevention/flu/index.html
• 이용 조건: Text: original wording based on facts from the CDC (U.S. federal work, public domain) and a Texas A&M official page (facts only). / Photo: public domain (U.S. Navy work), Philip A. McDaniel, File:US Navy 021019-N-9593M-007 Flu shot preparations.jpg
• 발행: 대표님 요청으로 승인 대기 없이 바로 발행'),
  ('college-station', 'published', 'College Station Adopts New Parks Master Plan to Guide 2027-2032 Projects', '<p>COLLEGE STATION — College Station has a new parks and recreation master plan for the first time since 2011.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs-ln-05.webp" alt="A U.S. Navy Band group performs at Veterans Park and Athletic Complex in College Station (file photo, 2017). It is not from the Oct. 22 council meeting. Photo: Chief Musician Adam Grimm, U.S. Navy / Wikimedia Commons (public domain)"><p><em>▲ A U.S. Navy Band group performs at Veterans Park and Athletic Complex in College Station (file photo, 2017). It is not from the Oct. 22 council meeting. Photo: Chief Musician Adam Grimm, U.S. Navy / Wikimedia Commons (public domain)</em></p><p>The City Council voted 6-1 on Thursday, Oct. 22, to adopt the 2027-2032 Parks and Recreation Master Plan and repeal the 2011 Parks, Recreation, and Open Spaces Master Plan, according to the city''s live blog of the meeting. Councilman David White voted against the motion.</p><p>Work on the update began in spring 2025. Parks and Recreation staff collected feedback through the summer and fall of 2025 at department events, scheduled pop-up events in parks and unscheduled visits to neighborhood parks, the city said.</p><p>In all, the department held 39 engagement opportunities that produced more than 850 unique public touchpoints, according to the city. The Parks and Recreation Advisory Board also provided input.</p><p>The city said residents expressed strong appreciation for current parks and programs and a desire to build on them. Staff also heard growing interest in connecting with nature, and environmental education and preserving natural spaces emerged as key themes.</p><p>The city''s blog did not list specific projects or costs tied to the new plan. Earlier in the meeting, the council approved other items, including a $750,000 U.S. Environmental Protection Agency community project grant for the Carter''s Grove utility rehabilitation project.</p><p>Residents can also see one of the city''s nature parks up close next month: the city announced Oct. 26 that its Lick Creek Park in the Dark overnight camping events return in November.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'The College Station City Council voted 6-1 on Oct. 22 to adopt a 2027-2032 Parks and Recreation Master Plan, replacing a plan from 2011 after more than a year of public input.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs-ln-05.webp', '2026-10-23T13:10:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['College Station','parks','master plan','City Council']::text[], '• 확인할 점: The plan document itself was not found or read; projects, priorities and costs are left out. Add highlights once the city posts the adopted plan.
• 확인할 점: Reason for Councilman David White''s no vote was not given in the city blog; not speculated.
• 확인할 점: Lick Creek Park in the Dark dates/details were not checked beyond the Oct. 26 headline; only mentioned as returning in November.
• 확인할 점: Live blog is ''not the official minutes''; confirm vote with minutes.
• 원문(City of College Station blog — Live from City Hall: Thursday''s City Council meeting (Oct. 22, 2026)): https://blog.cstx.gov/2026/10/22/live-from-city-hall-thursdays-city-council-meeting-oct-22/
• 원문(City of College Station blog — 5 things to watch at Thursday''s City Council meeting (Oct. 21, 2026)): https://blog.cstx.gov/2026/10/21/5-things-to-watch-at-thursdays-city-council-meeting-103/
• 원문(City of College Station — Pack your tents: Lick Creek Park in the Dark returns for November overnights (Oct. 26, 2026)): https://www.cstx.gov/news/posts/pack-your-tents-lick-creek-park-in-the-dark-returns-for-november-overnights/
• 이용 조건: Text: original reporting from City of College Station public blog posts (facts rewritten) / Photo: Public domain (U.S. Navy work), Chief Musician Adam Grimm, File:Navy Band visits College Station (35574057314).jpg
