  ('local-news', 'published', 'Bryan ISD Asks Voters to Approve 2-Cent Tax Rate Increase on Nov. 3 Ballot', '<p>BRYAN — Bryan Independent School District voters will decide Nov. 3 whether to raise the district''s maintenance and operations tax rate by about 2 cents, a measure the district says would bring in an estimated $5.5 million a year.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs-ln-07.webp" alt="A bilingual &quot;Vote Here&quot; sign at a polling place in Taft, Texas (file photo, 2016). It is not from Brazos County or the Nov. 3, 2026 election. Photo: Jay Phagan / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 2.0)"><p><em>▲ A bilingual "Vote Here" sign at a polling place in Taft, Texas (file photo, 2016). It is not from Brazos County or the Nov. 3, 2026 election. Photo: Jay Phagan / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 2.0)</em></p><p>The Bryan ISD Board of Trustees voted unanimously in August to call the voter-approval tax rate election, or VATRE, the district said. The measure would raise the maintenance and operations rate from $0.6769 to $0.6968 per $100 of taxable value.</p><p>On the ballot, the measure appears as Bryan Independent School District Proposition A and is labeled "This is a tax increase." It asks voters to ratify a total tax rate of $0.9668 per $100 valuation, which the ballot language says would raise maintenance and operations revenue by 2.10% over the previous year, or an additional $5,595,641.</p><p>The district said the extra money would not be subject to recapture, the state system that sends some local school tax revenue to other districts, so all of it would stay in Bryan ISD. A strategic planning committee of families, community members, staff and students identified three priorities for the money: competitive employee pay, protecting the district''s programs, and campus safety and security.</p><p>Unlike a bond, a VATRE does not create debt or pay for construction, the district said. The district also said it is committed to not raising its debt service tax rate, which pays off voter-approved bonds.</p><p>For a home with a $350,000 market value and about $210,000 in taxable value after the standard homestead exemption, the increase would add about $42 a year to the school tax bill, according to the district''s example. Homeowners 65 and older whose school taxes are frozen, and who have not improved their homes, would not be affected, the district said.</p><p>"As a Board, our commitment is to provide clear and transparent information related to the VATRE and its financial impact if approved by the voters," board President Joel Bryan said in the district''s August announcement.</p><p>Early voting ends Friday, Oct. 30. Brazos County voters in the district may vote at any county vote center; Robertson County voters in the district have separate polling sites, the district said.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'Bryan ISD''s Proposition A would raise the district''s maintenance and operations tax rate by about 2 cents, bringing in an estimated $5.5 million a year for pay, programs and safety.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs-ln-07.webp', '2026-10-25T11:05:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['Bryan ISD','VATRE','property tax','election','schools']::text[], '• 확인할 점: The district calls it a ''two-cent'' increase; the exact change is 1.99 cents ($0.6769 to $0.6968). Text says ''about 2 cents.''
• 확인할 점: Two revenue figures exist: ''estimated $5.5 million'' (district materials) and ''$5,595,641'' (ballot language). Both are attributed.
• 확인할 점: Bryan ISD trustee candidates (District 2, At-Large Place 6) were not confirmed from a readable district page and are left out. Board President Joel Bryan may be on the ballot himself; unverified.
• 확인할 점: The $42/year figure is the district''s illustration for one example home; actual impact varies.
• 확인할 점: Bryan ISD website pages render with JavaScript; text was read from the page data and the district''s article API (same content as the public pages).
• 원문(Bryan ISD — Bryan ISD Board of Trustees Calls for a Voter-Approval Tax Rate Election (VATRE) (Aug. 10, 2026)): https://www.bryanisd.org/article/3068670/
• 원문(Bryan ISD — 2026 VATRE information page (accessed Oct. 27, 2026)): https://www.bryanisd.org/page/vatre2026/
• 이용 조건: Text: original reporting from Bryan ISD public announcements and election information (facts rewritten; ballot language and one quote cited) / Photo: CC BY 2.0, Jay Phagan, File:Vote Here sign in Taft, Texas.jpg
