  ('latest', 'published', 'Bryan Releases Legal Memo on Texas Avenue Medians Vote as Early Voting Nears End', '<p>BRYAN — Bryan voters deciding a charter amendment that calls for removing medians on Texas Avenue now have a legal opinion from the city''s outside attorneys, which says the city probably could not carry out the change on its own even if the measure passes.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs-ln-01.webp" alt="The former Bryan Municipal Building in downtown Bryan, a historic city-built structure that now houses a children&#x27;s museum (file photo, 2014). It is not Bryan City Hall or Texas Avenue. Photo: Larry D. Moore / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 4.0)"><p><em>▲ The former Bryan Municipal Building in downtown Bryan, a historic city-built structure that now houses a children''s museum (file photo, 2014). It is not Bryan City Hall or Texas Avenue. Photo: Larry D. Moore / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 4.0)</em></p><p>The Bryan City Council voted after an executive session on Oct. 13 to release the otherwise confidential memo, waiving attorney-client privilege so residents could read it before the Nov. 3 election, the city said in a news release.</p><p>Proposition A would add a Section 35, titled "Texas Avenue Medians Removal," to the city charter. It calls for removing the medians on Texas Avenue from the Rosemary Drive intersection to about 600 feet south of State Highway 21, according to the city''s notice of special election. The city estimates the cost at $8 million and said it would have to find a funding source, which could limit spending on other capital projects.</p><p>The memo says the citizen petition behind the amendment was valid, so the council had to order the election. But it concludes that, whatever the outcome, "it is unlikely the City of Bryan has the legal authority to unilaterally make any modifications to the medians on Texas Avenue or to remove them completely." Texas Avenue is a state road controlled by the Texas Department of Transportation, and the memo says the city would probably violate the Texas Transportation Code if it acted without TxDOT''s written consent or an agreement handing control to the city.</p><p>If voters approve the amendment, the memo says, litigation or an opinion from the Texas attorney general may be needed to determine whether the city can comply.</p><p>The city said traffic data supports raised medians as a safety measure but acknowledged that the medians have changed daily routes and access to homes and businesses. Before the election, the city had already planned to seek funding for "loons" — wider paved areas that give drivers room to make U-turns — at intersections along Texas Avenue. City engineers are evaluating possible changes and working with TxDOT, the city said.</p><p>If the amendment passes, the city said removal would not happen quickly. Next steps could include legal review, talks with TxDOT, engineering and design, funding decisions and construction bidding.</p><p>Early voting runs through Friday, Oct. 30, and Election Day is Tuesday, Nov. 3. Bryan voters will also choose a mayor, and voters in Single Member Districts 1 and 2 will elect council members.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'An outside legal opinion released by Bryan says the city likely cannot remove Texas Avenue medians without TxDOT consent, even if voters approve Proposition A on Nov. 3.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs-ln-01.webp', '2026-10-26T07:40:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['Bryan','Texas Avenue','Proposition A','election','TxDOT']::text[], '• 확인할 점: Quote is from the memo summary as reprinted in the city''s Oct. 13 release; the full memo (PDF dated Oct. 7) was not read line by line.
• 확인할 점: The memo is the opinion of the city''s outside legal contractor; the firm is not named in the release, so it is not named here.
• 확인할 점: The notice of special election text in the PDF has OCR-style typos (''B1yan'', ''Rosemaiy''); street name written as Rosemary Drive per the city''s own descriptions.
• 확인할 점: Results come after polls close at 7 p.m. Nov. 3; update the story after the election. Bryan canvass is set for the Nov. 17 council meeting.
• 확인할 점: Photo is only a general Bryan civic-building file photo; replace with a Texas Avenue median photo if the newsroom has one.
• 원문(City of Bryan — City of Bryan Sharing External Legal Opinion for Medians Charter Amendment (Oct. 13, 2026)): https://www.bryantx.gov/news/posts/city-of-bryan-sharing-external-legal-opinion-for-medians-charter-amendment/
• 원문(City of Bryan — Notice of Special Election (Oct. 12, 2026; signed notice PDF)): https://www.bryantx.gov/news/posts/notice-of-special-election/
• 원문(City of Bryan — Notice of Special Election PDF): https://media-002-us.cdn.govstack.com/bryantx-004-us/media/4gmbxkvi/notice-of-special-election-2026-signed.pdf
• 원문(City of Bryan — November 2026 Voters Guide (Sept. 4, 2026)): https://www.bryantx.gov/news/posts/city-of-bryan-november-2026-voters-guide/
• 이용 조건: Text: original reporting from City of Bryan public releases and notices (facts rewritten; one quote from the released memo) / Photo: CC BY 4.0, Larry D. Moore, File:Former bryan municipal building 2014.jpg
