  ('health-sleep', 'published', 'Clocks Fall Back Sunday: Sleep Experts Share Tips for Adjusting to the Change', '<p>COLLEGE STATION — Brazos Valley residents will set their clocks back one hour this weekend when daylight saving time ends at 2 a.m. Sunday, Nov. 1. Sleep specialists say the extra hour is welcome but that the shift can still unsettle the body''s internal clock for a few days.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs-ls-01.webp" alt="An analog alarm clock on a bedroom desk (file photo, 2026; not taken in the Brazos Valley). Photo: Danielrayyan09 / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 4.0)"><p><em>▲ An analog alarm clock on a bedroom desk (file photo, 2026; not taken in the Brazos Valley). Photo: Danielrayyan09 / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY 4.0)</em></p><p>In 2026, daylight saving time ran from March 8 to Nov. 1, according to the National Institute of Standards and Technology, the federal agency that maintains official U.S. time. When clocks reach 2 a.m. Sunday, they go back to 1 a.m., so that hour happens twice. Under current federal rules, clocks will spring forward again on the second Sunday of March, which falls on March 14, 2027.</p><p>The American Academy of Sleep Medicine, a professional society for sleep doctors and researchers, published fall time-change tips Oct. 12. It recommends getting at least seven hours of sleep a night both before and after the change. It also suggests moving bedtime slightly later in the days before the switch, along with other daily cues such as meals and exercise.</p><p>The academy advises setting clocks to the new time Saturday evening, going to bed at the usual time and waking at the same time every day, weekends included. It recommends getting outdoors for early morning sunlight in the days after the change to help reset the internal clock. It also advises against extra caffeine late in the day and says children and teenagers may need more time to adjust, so their bedtime routines should stay steady.</p><p>Most people adjust within a few days, the academy says. People who regularly have trouble sleeping or feel tired during the day should talk with a doctor, it adds.</p><p>The time change can also affect driving. In a 2025 academy survey, 41% of U.S. adults said they had felt so sleepy behind the wheel that it affected their ability to drive safely. The academy lists warning signs that include frequent yawning, trouble remembering the last few miles, missing a turn and drifting into another lane. It advises pulling over or letting a passenger drive if any of these happen.</p><p>The National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute, part of the National Institutes of Health, offers year-round habits that also help during the change. It recommends going to bed and waking up at the same time every day, keeping weekend schedules within about an hour of weeknights and using the hour before bed for quiet time away from bright screens. It notes that caffeine''s effects can last up to eight hours, recommends a quiet, cool and dark bedroom, and says adults should nap no more than 20 minutes.</p><p>The academy supports ending seasonal clock changes in favor of permanent standard time, which it says lines up best with the body''s natural rhythms. In its Oct. 12 article, the academy said Congress is considering two bills. One would make daylight saving time permanent and the other would make standard time permanent.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'Daylight saving time ends at 2 a.m. Sunday, Nov. 1. The American Academy of Sleep Medicine recommends morning light, a steady wake time and at least seven hours of sleep.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs-ls-01.webp', '2026-10-26T08:15:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['daylight saving time','sleep','time change','drowsy driving','health']::text[], '• 확인할 점: DST end date (Sunday, Nov. 1, 2026, 2 a.m. local) and the March 14, 2027 restart are from the NIST page (2026 dates stated; 2027 calculated from the ''second Sunday of March'' rule). They would change only if Congress passes a new law.
• 확인할 점: The AASM tips page is dated Oct. 12, 2026. The bill numbers it cites (H.R. 139 Sunshine Protection Act, H.R. 9638 Sunshine for Our Kids Act) were left out of the text because their current status in Congress was not checked.
• 확인할 점: The 41% drowsy-driving figure comes from AASM''s 2025 survey, as reported in its Oct. 29, 2025 article. The AAA Foundation ''one in six fatal crashes'' figure was left out because it was not checked at the primary source.
• 확인할 점: CDC sleep pages (cdc.gov/sleep) returned Access Denied, so they were not used. NHLBI habits come from the NHLBI page (last updated March 24, 2022).
• 확인할 점: The photo is a generic bedroom clock taken in May 2026, location unknown. The caption says it is not local.
• 원문(NIST — Daylight Saving Time (DST) (updated Feb. 9, 2026)): https://www.nist.gov/pml/time-and-frequency-division/popular-links/daylight-saving-time-dst
• 원문(American Academy of Sleep Medicine (sleepeducation.org) — How to adjust when daylight saving time ends (Oct. 12, 2026)): https://sleepeducation.org/how-to-adjust-when-daylight-saving-time-ends/
• 원문(American Academy of Sleep Medicine — Falling back could leave you drowsy behind the wheel (Oct. 29, 2025)): https://sleepeducation.org/falling-back-could-leave-you-drowsy-behind-the-wheel/
• 원문(American Academy of Sleep Medicine — Daylight Saving Time position resource page): https://sleepeducation.org/resources/daylight-saving-time/
• 원문(NHLBI (NIH) — Healthy Sleep Habits (updated March 24, 2022)): https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/sleep-deprivation/healthy-sleep-habits
• 이용 조건: Text: original wording based on facts from NIST and NHLBI (U.S. federal works, public domain) and AASM public pages (facts only, nothing copied). / Photo: CC BY 4.0, Danielrayyan09, File:Red Desk Bedroom Analog Clock.jpg
• 발행: 대표님 요청으로 승인 대기 없이 바로 발행'),
  ('texas-am', 'published', 'Texas A&M to Build AI-Driven Lab to Speed Discovery of New Metal Alloys', '<p>COLLEGE STATION — Texas A&amp;M University plans to build a campus lab where artificial intelligence and robots design, make and test new metal alloys, the university announced Monday.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs-ln-06.webp" alt="The Zachry Engineering Education Complex at Texas A&amp;M University (file photo, 2022). The university has not said where the Aggie AI Foundry will be built. Photo: Oldag07 / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0)"><p><em>▲ The Zachry Engineering Education Complex at Texas A&amp;M University (file photo, 2022). The university has not said where the Aggie AI Foundry will be built. Photo: Oldag07 / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0)</em></p><p>A $2 million grant from the WoodNext Foundation to the Department of Materials Science and Engineering will create the Aggie AI Foundry, according to Texas A&amp;M. The foundation manages the philanthropy of Roku founder and CEO Anthony Wood, a 1987 graduate, and his wife, Susan, a 1989 graduate.</p><p>The university said the automated system is meant to cut the time needed to develop a new alloy from months to a week or less. It described the foundry as the first lab of its kind in academia, because existing self-driving labs work with materials that are easier to make and cannot handle designing and testing new metal alloys at temperatures up to 3,000 degrees Celsius.</p><p>The project was conceived by department head Ibrahim Karaman and associate department head for research Raymundo Arróyave. Under the plan, AI trained on physical models, algorithms and past research would propose new alloys, and robotic arms would mix, melt and test samples along an assembly line.</p><p>"Their investment allows us to pursue a bold idea: building an autonomous foundry where AI and robotics work together to accelerate the discovery of new alloys that could power the next generation of technologies," Karaman said in the university''s announcement.</p><p>Arróyave said the goal is to free students from repetitive lab work. "We want to free human brains and hands to conduct more science and write papers that share these discoveries with the world," he said.</p><p>The university said new metals and alloys are important to energy, aerospace, defense and health care. It did not say where on campus the foundry will be located or when it will open.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'A $2 million WoodNext Foundation grant will create the Aggie AI Foundry, a Texas A&M campus lab where AI and robots design and test new metal alloys, the university said.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs-ln-06.webp', '2026-10-26T15:20:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['Texas A&M','research','artificial intelligence','engineering']::text[], '• 확인할 점: Location on campus, opening date and staffing were not stated in the release.
• 확인할 점: Titles: Karaman is department head and Chevron Professor I; Arróyave is associate department head for research and Chevron Professor II (endowed titles shortened in text).
• 확인할 점: Release byline: Denise Brehm, Texas A&M College of Engineering.
• 원문(Texas A&M Stories — Texas A&M to create AI-powered foundry for faster alloy discovery (Oct. 26, 2026)): https://stories.tamu.edu/news/2026/10/26/texas-am-to-create-ai-powered-foundry-for-faster-alloy-discovery/
• 이용 조건: Text: original reporting from a Texas A&M University news release (facts rewritten; two quotes from the release) / Photo: CC BY-SA 4.0, Oldag07, File:TAMU-Zachry-Engineering-Education-Complex-1.jpg
• 발행: 대표님 요청으로 승인 대기 없이 바로 발행'),
  ('business', 'published', 'College Station Council Backs Zoning for 1,266-Acre Savannah Oaks Community', '<p>COLLEGE STATION — A 1,266-acre master-planned community in south College Station that city officials describe as the largest planned development in the city''s history cleared a key zoning step last week, the city announced Monday.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs-bz-01.webp" alt="Bluebonnets beside a highway near College Station (file photo, 2006; not the Savannah Oaks site). Photo: Mikecm / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 3.0)"><p><em>▲ Bluebonnets beside a highway near College Station (file photo, 2006; not the Savannah Oaks site). Photo: Mikecm / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 3.0)</em></p><p>The College Station City Council voted unanimously Oct. 22 to approve a zoning framework for Savannah Oaks, according to Grow College Station, the city''s economic development office. The developer is Silverfin Land Development LLC, a Corpus Christi-based company founded in 2015 that the city said has developed more than 50,000 acres across Texas.</p><p>The property lies off Highway 6 between the Pebble Creek and Southern Pointe neighborhoods. The master plan calls for about 2,688 single-family homesites, two townhouse sites and possible multifamily housing, along with a 162-acre mixed-use district along the highway that could hold retail, restaurants, offices, hotels and senior living. The city said the mix and timing of that district depend on future approvals.</p><p>The plan also sets aside more than 285 acres of parkland and open space and nearly 20 miles of trails and shared-use paths, including connections to the city''s 562-acre Lick Creek Park. About 11.59 acres are identified for a possible future school, subject to coordination with College Station ISD.</p><p>City and developer projections put the value of commercial development at $300 million to $350 million and estimate $1.6 billion to $2.05 billion in new taxable value once the project is fully built. The announcement did not say how many years full buildout would take.</p><p>The zoning allows single-family densities of up to eight lots per acre in some areas, but the city said that figure is neither the planned homesite count nor a projectwide cap. Final lot numbers will depend on later planning, infrastructure and subdivision approvals.</p><p>Work on first-phase infrastructure is expected to begin in the first quarter of 2027, subject to engineering, permitting and market conditions, with model homes and the first residents targeted for early summer 2028.</p><p>"Savannah Oaks is a rare opportunity to thoughtfully guide the next chapter of growth in south College Station," said Michael Ostrowski, the city''s chief development officer. Brantley Gwin, president of Silverfin Land Development, said, "We want Savannah Oaks to become a lasting part of College Station."</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'The College Station City Council approved zoning for Savannah Oaks, a 1,266-acre south College Station development with about 2,688 homesites and a 162-acre mixed-use district.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs-bz-01.webp', '2026-10-26T15:20:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['Savannah Oaks','College Station','development','housing','Grow College Station']::text[], '• 확인할 점: Source is the city/developer joint announcement posted Oct. 26, 2026; projections ($300M-$350M commercial value, $1.6B-$2.05B taxable value) are the city''s and developer''s estimates, not independent figures.
• 확인할 점: Council vote date Oct. 22 and ''unanimously'' come from the city post; confirm against the Oct. 22 council minutes once posted.
• 확인할 점: Builder lineup not yet announced; developer interest-list website deliberately left out of the body.
• 확인할 점: The release gives Lick Creek Park as 562 acres; the city''s visitor site lists 523 acres. Kept the release figure; editor may want to verify.
• 확인할 점: Photo is a 2006 roadside bluebonnet field near College Station, not the development site.
• 원문(City of College Station (Grow College Station) — Savannah Oaks will be the largest planned development in College Station history (Oct. 26, 2026)): https://grow.cstx.gov/news/posts/savannah-oaks-will-be-the-largest-planned-development-in-college-station-history/
• 이용 조건: Text: original writing based on a City of College Station public news release; quotes as published in the release / Photo: CC BY-SA 3.0, Mikecm, File:Bluebonnets College Station Texas.jpg
• 발행: 대표님 요청으로 승인 대기 없이 바로 발행'),
  ('aggie-football', 'published', 'Aggies Set for 2 p.m. Kickoff at South Carolina After Bye Week', '<p>COLLEGE STATION — Texas A&amp;M will play at South Carolina at 2 p.m. Central on Saturday, Nov. 7, with the game televised on ESPN, the Southeastern Conference announced Monday, according to Texas A&amp;M Athletics.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs-sp-02.webp" alt="Kyle Field at Texas A&amp;M University in College Station (file photo, 2022). Photo: Oldag07 / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0)"><p><em>▲ Kyle Field at Texas A&amp;M University in College Station (file photo, 2022). Photo: Oldag07 / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0)</em></p><p>The Aggies, 4-4 overall and 1-4 in SEC play, do not play this weekend. They are coming off a 31-20 loss at No. 13 Alabama on Oct. 24.</p><p>Texas A&amp;M is 10-2 all-time against South Carolina and 4-2 in Columbia, South Carolina, according to the athletics department. The Aggies lost their last two games at Williams-Brice Stadium, 44-20 in 2024 and 30-24 in 2022, and last won there 48-3 in 2020.</p><p>South Carolina is 4-3 after a 27-24 home win over No. 11 Tennessee and plays at Oklahoma this Saturday, the release said.</p><p>The rest of the regular season, as listed on the team''s official schedule, brings Tennessee to Kyle Field on Nov. 14 for the Operation Blackout game, with kickoff time still to be set. Texas A&amp;M then travels to Oklahoma on Nov. 21; the schedule lists that game as an early kickoff without a set time.</p><p>The regular season ends Friday, Nov. 27, when Texas visits Kyle Field for the Lone Star Showdown. The schedule lists a 6:30 p.m. kickoff on ABC, and the game is designated a Maroon Out game.</p><p>Punter Tyler White was named to the Ray Guy Award''s "Ray''s 8" list for Week 8 on Monday, his third selection this season, the athletics department said. White averaged 47.2 yards on four punts at Alabama and leads the SEC with an average of 49.4 yards per punt.</p><p>After the Alabama game, head coach Mike Elko said the team needs "to have some pride" over the final month. "We have to get a lot better as a football team," he said, according to remarks posted by Texas A&amp;M Athletics.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'The SEC set a 2 p.m. Central kickoff on ESPN for Texas A&M''s Nov. 7 game at South Carolina, the first of four November games that end with Texas at Kyle Field on Nov. 27.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs-sp-02.webp', '2026-10-26T16:40:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['Texas A&M football','South Carolina','Kyle Field','Lone Star Showdown','SEC schedule']::text[], '• 확인할 점: Kickoff times for the Nov. 14 Tennessee game (listed as ''Flex'') and the Nov. 21 Oklahoma game (listed as ''Early'') were not announced as of Oct. 27; update when the SEC announces them.
• 확인할 점: Nov. 27 Texas game time (6:30 p.m.) and ABC are from the 12thman.com schedule page, not a separate release; confirm before game week.
• 확인할 점: Elko quote fragment ''to have some pride'' comes from his printed postgame answer: ''we''ve got to have some pride.'' Rewritten as partial quote; editor may prefer the full sentence.
• 확인할 점: South Carolina''s record (4-3) and the Tennessee ranking (No. 11) are as stated in the Texas A&M release dated Oct. 26; not cross-checked on the South Carolina site (outside the allowed sources).
• 원문(Texas A&M Athletics — Kick Time Announced for South Carolina Game (Oct. 26, 2026)): https://12thman.com/news/2026/10/26/kick-time-announced-for-south-carolina-game2026
• 원문(Texas A&M Athletics — 2026 Football Schedule (accessed Oct. 27, 2026)): https://12thman.com/sports/football/schedule
• 원문(Texas A&M Athletics — White Named to Ray Guy Award''s "Ray''s 8" Week 8 (Oct. 26, 2026)): https://12thman.com/news/2026/10/26/white-named-to-ray-guy-awards-rays-8-week-8
• 원문(Texas A&M Athletics — Football Drops 31-20 Battle to No. 13 Alabama (Oct. 24, 2026)): https://12thman.com/news/2026/10/24/aggies-at-alabama
• 이용 조건: Text: original writing based on facts from Texas A&M Athletics releases; quotes as printed by Texas A&M Athletics / Photo: CC BY-SA 4.0, Oldag07, File:TAMU Kyle Field 2022-01-14 3.jpg (4032x3024)
