  ('latest', 'published', 'Early Voting Ends Friday in Brazos County: What''s on the Nov. 3 Local Ballot', '<p>BRYAN — Brazos County voters have until Friday, Oct. 30, to vote early in the Nov. 3 general election, which includes mayor''s races in both Bryan and College Station, school board seats and two local propositions.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs-ln-03.webp" alt="The Memorial Student Center at Texas A&amp;M University, one of five early voting sites in Brazos County (file photo, 2022). It does not show voting. Photo: Oldag07 / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0)"><p><em>▲ The Memorial Student Center at Texas A&amp;M University, one of five early voting sites in Brazos County (file photo, 2022). It does not show voting. Photo: Oldag07 / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 4.0)</em></p><p>Early voting runs from 7 a.m. to 7 p.m. each day through Friday, according to election notices published by the city of Bryan and College Station ISD. The early voting sites are the Brazos County Election Administrator''s Office and Arena Hall in Bryan, Galilee Baptist Church in Bryan, the College Station Utilities Meeting and Training Facility, and the Memorial Student Center on the Texas A&amp;M University campus.</p><p>On Election Day, 28 vote centers across the county will be open from 7 a.m. to 7 p.m. Brazos County uses countywide voting, so registered voters may cast a ballot at any vote center in the county, the city of Bryan said. Voters will be asked for photo identification, such as a driver license, passport, military ID or handgun license.</p><p>In Bryan, all city voters will choose a mayor between Rafael D. Peña III and Bobby Gutierrez and decide Proposition A, a citizen-initiated charter amendment on removing Texas Avenue medians. Single Member District 1 voters will choose between Chris Scotti and Paul Torres, and District 2 voters will choose among Chrissy Ransome, Jeannette Stewart Moseley and Ray Arrington, according to the city''s voters guide. A candidate needs more than 50% of the vote to win.</p><p>In College Station, the mayoral candidates are Bob Yancy and Elizabeth Cunha. Place 1 candidates are Mark Smith, Howard Mayne and Roy May, and Place 2 candidates are Chandler Arden and Michael Kidd. A special election for the unexpired Place 5 term lists Tre Watson, Padon Holt and Valen Cepak, according to the city''s elections page.</p><p>College Station ISD voters will fill at-large Places 6 and 7 on the school board, each for a three-year term. Michelle Gallagher and incumbent Kim Ege are running for Place 6, and incumbent Heather Simmen and Karen Strope are running for Place 7, the district said. Bryan ISD voters will decide a voter-approval tax rate election, listed as the district''s Proposition A, along with trustee races.</p><p>The city of Bryan said voters registered in another Texas county, such as college students, can request a limited ballot that includes only races shared with their home county. That option is available only during early voting at the Brazos County Elections Administration building.</p><p>Results for Bryan races will be posted after polls close at 7 p.m. on Election Day, the city said. All results remain unofficial until they are canvassed.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'Early voting for the Nov. 3 general election ends Oct. 30. Brazos County voters can use any vote center; local ballots include mayors, council seats, school boards and two propositions.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs-ln-03.webp', '2026-10-27T07:15:00-05:00'::timestamptz, array['election','Brazos County','early voting','Bryan','College Station']::text[], '• 확인할 점: The Brazos County Elections site (brazosvotes.org / elections.brazoscountytx.gov) returned HTTP 403 to our fetch, so all dates, hours and locations come from the City of Bryan election notices, the Bryan voters guide, the College Station elections page, CSISD''s election page and Bryan ISD''s VATRE page, which all match. Confirm against the county site before publishing.
• 확인할 점: Federal, state and county races on the same ballot were not confirmed from a primary source and are left out; the county sample ballot should be checked if the editor wants to add them.
• 확인할 점: Bryan ISD trustee candidate names were not confirmed from a readable district page and are left out.
• 확인할 점: Early-voting site room numbers differ between notices (MSC Room L526 for early voting vs. Room 2406 on Election Day in Bryan''s notice); room numbers left out.
• 확인할 점: After Oct. 30 this story becomes stale; swap the headline to Election Day information or results.
• 원문(City of Bryan — Notice of General Election (Oct. 12, 2026)): https://www.bryantx.gov/news/posts/notice-of-general-election/
• 원문(City of Bryan — November 2026 Voters Guide (Sept. 4, 2026)): https://www.bryantx.gov/news/posts/city-of-bryan-november-2026-voters-guide/
• 원문(City of College Station — Elections page (accessed Oct. 27, 2026)): https://www.cstx.gov/your-government/elections/
• 원문(College Station ISD — 2026 Election Information (accessed Oct. 27, 2026)): https://www.csisd.org/our-district/board/trustee-elections/2026-election-information
• 원문(Bryan ISD — 2026 VATRE page (accessed Oct. 27, 2026)): https://www.bryanisd.org/page/vatre2026/
• 이용 조건: Text: original reporting from public election notices by the cities of Bryan and College Station, CSISD and Bryan ISD (facts rewritten) / Photo: CC BY-SA 4.0, Oldag07, File:TAMU MSC 2022-02-14 1.jpg
• 발행: 대표님 요청으로 승인 대기 없이 바로 발행'),
  ('opinion', 'draft', 'Our View: Put the Full Price of Local Growth Deals in Plain Sight', '<p>COLLEGE STATION — Bryan and College Station are in the middle of a busy season of economic development deals, and this newspaper believes residents should be able to see, in one place and in plain numbers, what each of those deals costs and what the cities get in return.</p><img src="/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs-bz-05.webp" alt="A welcome sign in Bryan (file photo, 2014). Photo: Billy Hathorn / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 3.0)"><p><em>▲ A welcome sign in Bryan (file photo, 2014). Photo: Billy Hathorn / Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 3.0)</em></p><p>In September, the College Station City Council approved purchase and economic development agreements with Bass Pro Shops and Corinth Properties for 28 city-owned acres along State Highway 6. The city said it will provide Bass Pro an operations reimbursement, a sales tax reimbursement and permitting and infrastructure support, and Corinth an acquisition reimbursement in exchange for building and maintaining a public plaza. The city''s announcements did not list what those reimbursements are worth.</p><p>That same month, the Bryan City Council approved a 10-year agreement to restore two historic buildings on South Main Street. To its credit, Bryan''s public summary spelled out the developer''s deadlines: close on the buildings by Dec. 1, start work on one by April 1, 2027, and on the other by Sept. 1, 2027. But it, too, left out the dollar value of the city''s commitment.</p><p>The stakes are rising. College Station''s October sales tax payment from the state was up 8.79% from a year earlier and Bryan''s up 8.08%, but both trailed the 11.3% increase for Texas cities overall. When a city agrees to give back part of the sales tax a new store generates, that is real money that would otherwise help pay for police, parks and streets. And with projects like the 1,266-acre Savannah Oaks community projected to add $1.6 billion to $2.05 billion in taxable value, the decisions made now will shape city budgets for decades.</p><p>Many of these deals may well be good bargains. The details are usually available somewhere in council agenda packets, but few residents have time to dig through them. We urge both cities to publish a simple annual scorecard of every active incentive agreement: what the city pays or forgoes, for how long, what the company promised, and whether it has delivered. Bryan''s habit of listing deadlines is a start. Both cities can do more.</p><p><em>※ This article was written with the help of AI, based on public and official sources.</em></p>', 'Draft editorial: Bryan and College Station are signing incentive deals as growth accelerates. Residents deserve one plain-language scorecard of what each deal costs and requires.', '/sites/breakingbcs/photos/bcs-bz-05.webp', null::timestamptz, array['editorial','economic development','incentives','Bryan','College Station']::text[], '• 확인할 점: AI DRAFT: This is an AI-written draft editorial. The editor must rewrite it in the paper''s own voice and decide the position before publishing. Do not publish as is.
• 확인할 점: Claim that the cities'' announcements did not list incentive dollar amounts is based on the College Station posts of Aug. 27 and Sept. 24, 2026 and Bryan''s Oct. 15, 2026 summary; the amounts may appear in council agenda packets — editor should check and, if available, cite them (which would strengthen or change the argument).
• 확인할 점: ''Many of these deals may well be good bargains'' and the scorecard proposal are opinion, not fact.
• 확인할 점: Sales tax figures from Texas Comptroller data (see bcs-bz-03). The line that sales tax helps pay for police, parks and streets is general background.
• 확인할 점: Bryan approval date ''Sept. 15'' year inferred as 2026 from context (see bcs-bz-02).
• 원문(City of College Station (Grow College Station) — City Council approves agreements with Bass Pro Shops, Corinth Properties (Sept. 24, 2026)): https://grow.cstx.gov/news/posts/city-council-approves-agreements-with-bass-pro-shops-corinth-properties/
• 원문(City of Bryan — 2026 Full of Economic Development Wins for the City of Bryan & Residents (Oct. 15, 2026)): https://www.bryantx.gov/news/posts/2026-full-of-economic-development-wins-for-the-city-of-bryan-residents/
• 원문(Texas Comptroller — Monthly sales tax allocations release (Oct. 8, 2026)): https://comptroller.texas.gov/about/media-center/news/20261008-comptroller-huffines-distributes-13-billion-in-monthly-sales-tax-revenue-to-local-governments-1791392487613
• 원문(Texas Comptroller via data.texas.gov — City-County Comparison Summary, Brazos County cities, 2026): https://data.texas.gov/resource/53pa-m7sm.json?$where=county=''Brazos'' AND report_year=''2026''
• 원문(City of College Station — Savannah Oaks announcement (Oct. 26, 2026)): https://grow.cstx.gov/news/posts/savannah-oaks-will-be-the-largest-planned-development-in-college-station-history/
• 이용 조건: Text: original opinion draft based on public government sources / Photo: CC BY-SA 3.0, Billy Hathorn, File:Bryan, TX welcome sign IMG 4444.JPG
• 상태: 의견 기사라 작성 중(초안). 편집장이 고쳐 쓴 뒤 발행')
) as v(section, status, title, body, excerpt, thumb, published_at, tags, notes)
join categories c on c.outlet_id = ctx.outlet_id and c.slug = v.section
where not exists (select 1 from articles a where a.outlet_id = ctx.outlet_id and a.title = v.title);

-- 확인 (섹션별 기사 수·상태·기자명)
select c.name as "섹션", a.byline as "기자명", a.status as "상태", count(*) as "기사 수"
from articles a join categories c on c.id = a.category_id join outlets o on o.id = a.outlet_id
where o.name = 'Breaking BCS'
group by 1, 2, 3 order by 1;
